{
  "result": "The city library had a drawer labeled THINGS FOUND IN BOOKS. Inside were the usual bookmarks, a few grocery lists, and a photograph of a dog sitting beside a birthday cake. On the back, someone had written, \u201cHe knew it was his.\u201d\n\nMara worked the Thursday closing shift. When the library was empty, she sometimes opened the drawer and read one thing. Her favorite was a scrap of graph paper that said, \u201cTell Ruth about the moon.\u201d She had found it in a manual for repairing washing machines.\n\nOne evening, a man came in ten minutes before closing and asked whether anyone had found a photograph. Mara pulled open the drawer. He looked at the dog for a long time before picking it up.\n\n\u201cThat\u2019s my mother\u2019s handwriting,\u201d he said. \u201cShe used to bake him a cake every year. Absolutely vile. Peanut butter and sardines.\u201d\n\n\u201cDid he eat it?\u201d\n\n\u201cHe ate the candles once. We had to start counting them afterward.\u201d\n\nHe slipped the photograph into his coat pocket. At the door he took it out again and held it by the edges all the way down the steps.\n\nMara turned off the lights, then turned one back on. She took the graph paper from the drawer and looked up the washing machine manual. It was still on the shelf, though nobody had borrowed it in six years. She tucked the note between the pages on replacing a drive belt. If Ruth ever came looking, she knew where to start.",
